course: biology
lesson: 197
title: Cats: purring, whiskers, night eyes and grooming
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A pet cat is a small hunter that lives on your sofa, and almost everything about its body is built for creeping up on prey in dim light.
explain: Its whiskers are feelers that tell it whether a gap is wide enough, its eyes shine in headlights because a mirror layer inside them catches every bit of light, and its tongue is a comb.
explain: Stroke a cat on your lap and you feel a low rumble through its body: the purr. It usually means a happy cat, but cats also purr when they are hurt or frightened.
explain: A cat that sleeps all afternoon is not lazy: like its wild relatives it saves energy for the hunting hours at dawn and dusk.
fact: A cat purrs by making the muscles of its voice box twitch very fast as it breathes in and out, which makes a rumbling vibration.
fact: Cats purr when content, but also when hurt, ill or scared, perhaps to calm themselves; a mother cat purrs while feeding, which seems to keep the kittens close and calm, and kittens can purr when only a few days old.
fact: Whiskers are thick, deep-rooted hairs full of nerves; a cat uses them to judge whether it fits through a gap, and they must never be cut.
fact: When a cat pounces its whiskers swing forward to feel the prey close up, and cats also have small whiskers on the backs of their front legs.
fact: A cat's eyes shine green or yellow in headlights because a mirror layer at the back of the eye bounces light through again, so cats see in about one sixth of the light we need, but not in total darkness.
fact: In bright sun a cat's pupils shrink to thin slits to let in very little light, and in the dark they open into huge circles.
fact: Cats spend a large part of their waking time grooming, and their tongues are covered in tiny backward-pointing hooks that work like a comb.
fact: Fur swallowed while grooming can form a clump that the cat coughs up as a hairball; long-haired cats get more, and brushing helps.
fact: Cats sleep about twelve to sixteen hours a day and are most active at dawn and dusk, when their prey is too.
fact: When a cat rubs its cheek on your legs it is marking you with scent from glands in its face, as if saying «you belong to my group».
fact: Cats scratch to keep their claws in good shape and to leave scent from glands in their paws; a scratching post near the spot they like can save the sofa.
fact: Adult cats rarely meow at each other and talk to other cats mostly with smells, looks and body; they meow mostly to people, and each cat develops its own meows for its own family.
exercise: How does a cat make its purr? | Muscles in its voice box twitch very fast as it breathes | It hums through its nose with its mouth shut tight, like a person humming a tune | It rubs its back teeth together very quickly | Its tummy rumbles from food
exercise: Does a purring cat always feel happy? | No, cats also purr when hurt or scared | Yes, purring always means a cat is happy and well | Yes, only happy cats can purr | No, purring always means hungry
exercise: Why does a mother cat purr while feeding her kittens? | The kittens feel the purr and it keeps them close and calm | It scares other cats away | It warms the milk inside her body before the kittens drink | It tells the kittens to go to sleep forever
exercise: What do a cat's whiskers do when they touch the sides of a gap in a fence? | Help it judge whether it will fit | Make it sneeze | Sharpen themselves | Tell it what time it is and when dinner is coming
exercise: Why should you never cut a cat's whiskers? | They are part of its sense of touch | They grow back as claws | They keep its ears warm | They help it purr
exercise: Why do a cat's whiskers point forward when it pounces? | To feel the prey close up | To look scary | To keep its nose dry | To help it fly
exercise: Why do a cat's eyes shine green in car headlights? | A mirror layer at the back of the eye reflects the light | Cats have little lights in their eyes which switch on at night | Their eyes are made of glass | They are angry at the car
exercise: Can a cat see in total darkness? | No, it needs a little light, but far less than we do | Yes, cats see perfectly well in total darkness | No, cats are blind at night | Yes, but only in colour, while in the day they see in grey
exercise: Why do a cat's pupils become thin slits in bright sun? | To let in very little light | To help it fall asleep in the sun | Because it is angry about the light | To see colours better in the sun
exercise: Why does a cat lick its fur for so long? | It is grooming, and its tongue works like a comb | It is very hungry | It is tasting the sofa | It is trying to get wet all over so it can cool down in the heat
exercise: Why does a cat cough up a hairball? | Fur it swallowed while grooming forms a clump | It ate a whole ball of wool | It has a cold | It swallowed a mouse whole without chewing it at all
exercise: Which cats get more hairballs? | Long-haired cats | Kittens with no fur | Cats that never groom | Black cats only, never white ones
exercise: How many hours a day do cats usually sleep? | About twelve to sixteen | About two | About six | All twenty-four
exercise: When are cats most active? | At dawn and dusk | Only at midday, in the heat | Only at midnight, in the dark | Never, they always sleep
exercise: Why does a cat rub its cheek on your legs? | To mark you with its scent | To clean its face on your jeans | To push you away from its food bowl | Because its cheek itches a lot
exercise: Why does a cat scratch the sofa? | To keep its claws in shape and leave scent marks | To dig for the mice it thinks are hiding under the cushions | Because it hates the sofa and wants it gone from the house | To make a bed
exercise: Who do adult cats meow at most? | People | Other cats | Birds outside | Dogs in the house
test: «my cat's purring at the vet so he's fine, yeah?» What is right? | Not always, cats also purr when hurt or scared | Yes, purring means he is well | Yes, sick cats never purr | No, purring means he is about to bite
test: «his whiskers are dead long, I'll trim them like a haircut» What is right? | No, whiskers are feelers and must never be cut | Yes, it makes him faster and helps him climb | Yes, whiskers grow back in a day, just like your hair grows in a week | No, but cut half of them
test: «cats can see in pitch black, that's why they hunt at night» What is closer to the truth? | They need a little light, but only about a sixth of what we need | Yes, they see perfectly in total darkness, even in a cellar with no windows | No, cats are blind at night | Yes, they use night goggles
test: «ugh the cat's sicked up a hairy sausage on my bed» What is it most likely? | A hairball of swallowed fur from grooming | A mouse tail | A piece of carpet it ate when nobody was watching | A worm it found
test: «lazy cat sleeps all day, does nothing» What is the real reason? | Cats save energy for hunting at dawn and dusk | Cats are ill if they sleep in the day | Cats are always bored | Cats sleep only in winter, when it is too cold to go out and hunt
test: «Mittens rubbed her face all over my legs, she's itchy?» What is she doing? | Marking you with scent as part of her group | Wiping off her dinner | Trying to trip you up | Cleaning your jeans
test: What would happen if a cat had no scratching post anywhere at home? | It would probably scratch the furniture | Its claws would fall off within a few weeks | It would stop scratching for ever and forget how | It would learn to file its own nails on the floor
test: In a cartoon a cat meows to talk with other cats all night. What is true of real adult cats? | They meow mostly to people and talk to cats with smells, looks and body | They meow only to other cats, and stay completely silent with people all their lives | They never make any sound | They bark at each other
test: Count them: a mother cat has 5 kittens. How many eyes in the basket, mother included? | 12 | 10 | 6 | 24
test: Count them: 3 cats sleep in a row. How many front legs with little whiskers on the back? | 6 | 3 | 12 | 9
test: «why'd her eyes go all thin like a snake's in the sun» What is the answer? | Slit pupils let in very little light | She is cross with you | She is going blind | She is about to sneeze
test: «cat's eyes were glowing green in the dark, proper demon» What is the real reason? | A mirror layer at the back of each eye bounces the light back | Cats have little lights in their eyes that they switch on in the dark | It is a sign of illness | Only magic cats do that
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the purring of the domestic cat? | Purring may accompany contentment, injury or fear alike | Purring invariably indicates contentment | Purring is produced solely by the stomach | Only adult cats are capable of purring
exam: Identify the principal function of the whiskers of the domestic cat. | To furnish tactile information concerning gaps and nearby prey | To regulate the temperature of the ears during the coldest months of the year | To produce the sound of purring | To serve as decoration only
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the vision of the cat in conditions of low illumination. | A reflective layer permits vision in a fraction of the light required by humans, though not in total darkness | The cat perceives perfectly in total darkness, its eyes requiring no light whatsoever to form an image | The cat is entirely blind after sunset | The eyes of the cat emit light of their own
exam: It is given that a domestic cat sleeps for the greater part of the day. Ascertain the most accurate explanation. | Energy is conserved for hunting at dawn and dusk, when prey is active | The animal is invariably unwell | The animal is incapable of remaining awake, its brain lacking the required strength | Sleep is required solely in the winter months, when hunting is impossible
